  :adr/body "## Context

Owner direction, 2026-08-21: put aiueos in the position Linux, macOS, Windows
and Ubuntu hold, and split `grant` out into its own repository with its own
responsibility.

The premise was measured before acting on it, and it held. `aiueos` named two
things at once -- ADR-2608139980's own existence-name table says so in one
line: *aiueos / あいうえお / どの手を貸すか (grant + OS)*. Measured at the
split the two halves were nearly the same size: about 6,430 lines of decision
against about 6,070 lines of mechanism plus 7,054 lines of C and assembly.
One repository cannot be dependency-minimal and own a kernel.

Two findings came out of the measurement and shaped the result.

**1. `kotoba-lang/authority` already existed.** The capability lattice --
scopes as segment paths, `covers?` as a partial order, `meet` as attenuation,
zero runtime dependencies, no crypto. Creating a lattice would have put a
third answer on a plane ADR-2608180200 says must have one.

**2. `verify-delegation-surfaces` was not looking at aiueos.** The detector
that exists to find a second covering relation names `aiueos` in its
`owns-the-comparison` exemption set, and aiueos did not depend on
`kotoba-lang/authority` -- it had `src/aiueos/authority.cljc`, whose
`resource-match?` answers *does this grant cover this intent* by exact
membership plus a `:*` wildcard over `[action resource]` pairs. So there were
two answers, and one of them was exempt from the check by name.

## Decision

**Three planes, one direction.**

| repo | owns |
|---|---|
| `kotoba-lang/authority` | the lattice: scope algebra, `covers?`, `meet`, chains |
| `kotoba-lang/grant` | the broker: policy, admission, manifests, signing, surfaces, audit, release and enrolment decisions |
| `kotoba-lang/aiueos` | the machine: loader, kernel, paging, virtio, VFIO, hypervisor boundary, PID 1, the Wasm host, boot and release |

`aiueos` depends on `grant`; `grant` does not depend on `aiueos`. The cut
needed no rewiring to hold: measured at the split the require graph had zero
edges from the decision plane into the machine. The boundary was already true
and merely unstated, and `deps.edn` now states it.

**`aiueos` is an operating system, said plainly and then qualified.** Its
README names the category -- Linux, Windows, macOS -- and then spends more
words on where it actually is than on what it is for, because the honest
answer is uneven: firmware-to-ring-0-Kotoba works, a bounded six-page C-free
paging slice with W^X and real CPU fault receipts works, boot and release
media work in detail, and kernel execution, hardware and desktop are not
C-free at all yet. Every gate is QEMU/OVMF; there is no real-machine
qualification. x86-64 is the only ISA with a bare-metal kernel, and there is
no Android, iOS, Raspberry Pi or IoT profile.

**The contract vocabulary did not move.** `:aiueos.policy/*`,
`:aiueos.broker/*` and `:aiueos/*` keys, and the classpath paths that load
them, are unchanged. Those keys are on the wire -- kototama's adapter and
stored decisions read them -- so only code namespaces were renamed. Same call
as ADR-2608147300, where the pin registry became genpon and `:fleet/repos`
stayed.

**The lattice unification is NOT part of this split.** `grant.authority`
still holds the second covering relation. Folding it into `authority.scope`
is a semantic change, not a refactor: the lattice takes `kotoba://` scope
strings where this takes action/resource pairs. It needs its own evidence and
its own ADR. What moved with the code is the exemption -- the detector now
names `grant` instead of `aiueos` -- so the gap stays visible rather than
quietly becoming somebody else's.

## The rename had two silent-failure shapes, and the baseline is what caught them

Renaming `aiueos.X` to `grant.X` was guarded against rewriting keyword
namespaces by skipping any occurrence preceded by `:`. That guard has two
holes, because a keyword namespace can appear with no leading colon:

- **`:keys [aiueos.graph/order]`** -- destructuring writes the namespace bare.
  The rename rewrote the reader while the writer kept emitting the old key, so
  three assertions in `graph_test` started reading `nil`. Three sites.
- **`(= \"aiueos.policy\" (namespace k))`** -- comparing a keyword namespace as
  a string. Two sites in `contract.cljc`, and this one mattered more: it
  turned a fail-closed check into one that admitted every key it was written
  to reject.

Both were found only because the aiueos baseline was measured green first
(27 tests, 0 failures) before anything moved, so 9 failures and 2 errors in
the first grant run could not be dismissed as pre-existing. Both went red
with the defect and green without it.

## Verification

- **grant**: 291 tests, 918 assertions, 0 failures. The first run was 292 /
  920 with 9 failures and 2 errors; the difference of one test and two
  assertions is the deftest that moved back to aiueos.
- **aiueos**: 371 tests, 8,580 assertions, 0 failures. The first post-split
  run was 11 failures and 1 error, each traced to a named consequence.
- `verify-west-pins`: `grant` pin `7ed742ba` reachable from main; the entry is
  a 5-line minimal diff.

**Not verified**: the full aiueos suite was never run before the split. The
pre-split baseline is a 27-test spot check of `graph` and `contract`, not
8,580 assertions. The post-split green is therefore evidence that the split
converged, not that nothing regressed outside those two namespaces.

## What the TCB records had to do, and why it was not optional

`aiueos.tcb` is a per-file SHA-256 inventory of code whose compromise can
grant authority. It fail-closed on the split, which is the correct behaviour
and is the reason the next three items are edits rather than deletions.

- **19 file entries left `:tcb/files` and one coordinate entered
  `:tcb/external`.** A file digest and a git SHA are both content addresses;
  what changed is granularity. Deleting the entries without adding the
  coordinate would have shrunk the recorded TCB while the real one stayed the
  same size, and `tcb_test` now asserts both halves so that move cannot be
  made in one step.
- **`build-identity.edn` gained `:mechanism-external`, and `aiueos.tcb` was
  extended to require its coordinate be pinned in `:tcb/external`.** Two
  adopted properties -- `:digest-gated-admission` and
  `:declarative-configuration-deny-by-default` -- had mechanisms that moved.
  Deleting those paths from `:mechanism` would have left both properties
  claiming adoption with a shorter list that passes: a check that can no
  longer reach the code returning the same value as one that reached it and
  was satisfied.
- **`security-adoption.edn` narrowed from seven control namespaces to one.**
  `aiueos.policy` and `aiueos.audit` were the entrypoints exercising abac,
  crypto-policy, hardware, information-flow, transport and redaction, and both
  left.

## Named gaps

1. **`grant` has no TCB inventory and no security-adoption record.** It
   exercises six security control namespaces and declares none of them. The
   checker (`aiueos.tcb`) stayed with the machine. An unchecked record in
   grant would be exactly the decoration aiueos ADR-0067 warns about, so
   nothing was written rather than something unread.
2. **Three grant namespaces ship with no in-repository test**: `manifest`,
   `key-lifecycle` and `deployment-profile`. Their tests reach into
   `aiueos.execute`, `aiueos.launcher` and `aiueos.reproducibility`, so they
   stayed and run as cross-repository integration tests.
3. **Three contract entries name files in the other repository** --
   `src/aiueos/vm.cljc`, `src/aiueos/pid1.cljc` and
   `src/aiueos/provider/cloud.clj`, under `:enforced-by`, `:read-at-boot-by`
   and `:consumed-by`. Nothing checks those paths for existence, which is
   exactly why they are named here.
4. **The second covering relation**, above.

## Consequences

- ADR-2608139980's existence-name table is amended: `aiueos` is the machine,
  and `grant` is what decides which hand it lends.
- `abi` left aiueos's direct dependencies with the two namespaces that used
  it, and arrives transitively through grant.
- Nothing about the kotoba / amu / kototama / murakumo split changes.
